# XCU strings
# Copyright (c) 2026 Nicholas Martin. All rights reserved.
#
# Every test gives -a, since without it the part of a file that is scanned
# is implementation-defined. Test files hold only NUL, newline and ASCII
# printable bytes, so that no run ends at a byte whose treatment the page
# does not state. The page allows additional implementation-defined strings,
# so the tests look for the strings they expect and for the absence of
# strings that are too short, not for the exact output.

assertion  XCU.strings.001
anchor     utilities/strings.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     utilities/strings.html#STDOUT
class      required
since      1992
says       strings writes each run of four or more printable characters that
           is ended by a newline or a NUL as one line, and nothing shorter.
test       t/001-default-length.sh

assertion  XCU.strings.002
anchor     utilities/strings.html#OPTIONS/-n
class      required
since      1992
says       strings -n number makes number the shortest run written.
test       t/002-minimum-length.sh

assertion  XCU.strings.003
anchor     utilities/strings.html#OPTIONS/-t
anchor     utilities/strings.html#STDOUT
class      required
since      1992
says       strings -t d, -t o and -t x write each string preceded by its byte
           offset from the start of the file, in decimal, octal or
           (lowercase) hexadecimal, and one space.
test       t/003-offsets.sh

assertion  XCU.strings.004
anchor     utilities/strings.html#OPTIONS/-a
class      required
since      1992
says       strings -a scans the whole file: a string 131072 bytes in is found.
test       t/004-whole-file.sh

assertion  XCU.strings.005
anchor     utilities/strings.html#OPERANDS
anchor     utilities/strings.html#EXIT STATUS
class      required
since      1992
says       strings handles file operands in order, reads standard input with no
           operand, and exits nonzero for a file it cannot read.
test       t/005-operands.sh
